<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dashavatara Cave</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Ellora Cave 15</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dashavatara Cave, Cave 15 at Ellora in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, is a two-storeyed rock-cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple of the eighth century, named after its sculptures of the ten avatars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. It also has large Shaiva reliefs. An inscription of the Rashtrakuta founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a> carved in the cave records his conquests and is one of the earliest documents of the dynasty's rise.</p></div>
